<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puster Valley</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pustertal, Val Pusteria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Puster Valley is a long east–west Alpine valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-tyrol">East Tyrol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, drained by the Rienz and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a>. It was granted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> in 1091 and later came under the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruneck">Bruneck</a> is its main town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puster-valley.jpg" alt="Puster Valley" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Federer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Pustertal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
